  "asr_text": "Coffee prices are falling. Great. Maybe spring will finally come to my wallet too. But on supermarket shelves, the price tags do not move at all. They look perfectly calm, as if to say, News? Never heard of it. If the market price of beans has fallen, why aren't our costs getting any lower? First, let's go back to when high prices were causing a stir in the market. Bad weather in Brazil and Vietnam disrupted supply, leaving few beans available for sale and stocks low. Then came strong global demand. It was a tough mix for prices to settle down. Around the same time, ICE's Arabica coffee futures hit a record high up to that point. But futures prices are not the prices of green beans for immediate sale. And they are not store prices either. They may all wear the same name tag, coffee price, but they are not all the same thing.",
